ective than central squares.\n\nThe knight on f2 is my most active piece and should be used to maximum advantage. Moving it to e4 centralizes it perfectly, where it attacks c5, d2, f6, and g5, controls critical squares, and is difficult to dislodge. This move maximizes piece activity and creates multiple threats.\n\nReason: The knight move to e4 is strategically superior because:\n- It occupies the most powerful central square available\n- It attacks the advanced white pawn on c5\n- It creates potential tactical threats against White's position\n- It's difficult for White to challenge this centralized knight\n- It maintains maximum flexibility for my future plans\n\nAnswer: N: f2-e4"}
