8-h4**, which:\n- Attacks the knight on h2\n- Threatens checkmate patterns on f2\n- Forces White to respond defensively\n- Develops the queen with tempo\n\nReason: Q: d8-h4 is the most forcing move available. It creates immediate tactical threats against White's underdeveloped kingside, attacks the h2 knight, and threatens the weak f2 square. White will be forced to defend, giving me time to continue development with initiative. This aggressive approach is justified given that White's king is still in the center and their pieces are uncoordinated.\n\nAnswer: Q: d8-h4"}
