\n1 |    |RWF1|    |    |    |    |    |RWF0| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, P: c7-c6, K: c8-b8, K: c8-d7, P: d4-d3, R: d8-d7, R: d8-d6, R: d8-d5, R: d8-e8, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: f8-e7, B: f8-d6, B: f8-c5, B: g2-f1, B: g2-h1, B: g2-f3, B: g2-e4, B: g2-d5, B: g2-c6, B: g2-h3, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as Black. Looking at the board, I notice my king is on c8 and has moved. White's king is on b2, also moved. Material appears roughly balanced with both sides having rooks, bishops, and pawns. White has a knight on h2. The position is in the middlegame phase with some pawn structure complications. White's king position on b2 looks somewhat exposed.\n\nKey observations:\n- My bishop on g2 is very active, deep in White's position\n- White's king on b2 is relatively exposed with limited escape squares\n- My rook on d8 is centralized and active\n- White has pawns advanced on the queenside (a3, b4, c5)\n- My d4 pawn is quite advanced and passed\n\nEvaluation:\n1. **B: g2-c6** - Maintains pressure, controls key squares, but somewhat passive\n2. **B: g2-e4** - Centralizes the bishop, controls important squares, puts pressure on White's position\n3. **d4-d3** - Pushes the passed pawn closer to promotion, very dangerous and forcing White to respond\n4. **R: d8-d5** - Attacks the c5 pawn, centralizes the rook\n5. **B: g2-d5** - Centralizes bishop on a strong square, controls many key squares\n\nThe move **d4-d3** is extremely forcing. It threatens d2, putting enormous pressure on White. The pawn is only two squares from promotion and White will have difficulty stopping it. This creates immediate tactical threats and forces White into a defensive posture.\n\nReason: The passed d-pawn push to d3 is the most forcing and dangerous move. It threatens to advance to d2 next turn, creating a devastating passed pawn just one square from promotion. White's king on b2 and rook on b1 will be tied down to stopping this pawn, giving me a significant strategic advantage. This move maximizes winning chances by creating concrete threats.\n\nAnswer: P: d4-d3"}
