ing to move (likely Ka2, Kc2, or Kc1), disrupting White's position and maintaining initiative.\n\nReason: B: g2-f1+ is the strongest move because:\n- It forces White's king to move, giving me the initiative\n- It maintains my bishop's aggressive placement\n- In endgames, checks and forcing moves often lead to concrete advantages\n- It limits White's options and keeps my pieces active\n- After the king moves, I can continue improving my position with my extra material advantage\n\nAnswer: B: g2-f1"}
