\n1 |KWF1|    |    |    |    |    |    |RWF1| 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a7-a5, P: a7-a6, P: b7-b5, P: b7-b6, B: c2-b1, B: c2-d1, B: c2-b3, B: c2-a4, B: c2-d3, B: c2-e4, B: c2-f5, B: c2-g6, P: c7-c5, P: c7-c6, K: c8-b8, K: c8-d7, K: c8-d8, P: f7-f5, P: f7-f6, B: f8-e7, B: f8-d6, B: f8-c5, B: f8-b4, P: g7-g5, P: g7-g6, P: h7-h5, P: h7-h6, R: h8-g8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as Black in what appears to be an endgame position. Material count shows:\n- White: King on a1, Rook on h1, Knight on h2, Bishop on c2, and 3 pawns (a4, b4, g4)\n- Black: King on c8, Rook on h8, Bishop on f8, and 6 pawns (a7, b7, c7, f7, g7, h7)\n\nI have a material advantage with more pawns and my pieces are relatively undeveloped but safe. White's king is tucked in the corner on a1, which is somewhat exposed. My rook on h8 and White's rook on h1 are facing each other on the h-file.\n\nEvaluation: Let me consider several promising moves:\n\n1. **R: h8-g8** - Moves my rook off the h-file, potentially preparing to invade White's position via the g-file or supporting a kingside pawn advance.\n\n2. **K: c8-d7** - Activates my king toward the center, a crucial endgame principle. This improves king activity for supporting my pawn majority.\n\n3. **B: f8-d6** - Develops the bishop to a central square, controlling important squares and preparing to coordinate with other pieces.\n\n4. **P: c7-c5** - Advances a central pawn, gaining space and potentially supporting a queenside pawn majority push.\n\n5. **K: c8-b8** - Moves king but toward the side rather than center, less ideal.\n\nReason: In this endgame with a material advantage, I should activate my pieces, particularly my king. **K: c8-d7** is the strongest move because:\n- It centralizes my king (fundamental endgame principle)\n- Prepares to support my pawn advances\n- Improves king safety while maintaining flexibility\n- Keeps all my pieces coordinated for future plans\n- With my extra pawns, activating the king is key to converting the advantage\n\nAnswer: K: c8-d7"}
