op on an excellent diagonal, controlling key squares and supporting potential pawn advances.\n\nReason: Moving the bishop to c5 accomplishes multiple objectives: it activates my most passive piece, controls the important central diagonal, puts pressure on White's position, and prepares for potential pawn breaks with f7-f5 or advancement of central pawns. This maintains my material advantage while improving piece coordination. The bishop on c5 also helps control squares around White's exposed king.\n\nAnswer: B: f8-c5"}
